    watchPoints: ["Locations and approval schedules for at least 100,000 homes on new sites", "Annual progress toward the 2030 construction-start target", "Stage-by-stage progress and delays across Seoul’s 253 renewal districts", "Actual net additions after demolition", "Time from construction start to completion and occupancy", "Effects of redevelopment relocation on nearby rental markets"],
    quote: "When government announces a number, citizens should ask what it means — and later ask whether that number became reality.",
    sourceNote: "Prepared by comparing the central government’s rapid-supply package, the Ministry of Land’s follow-up explanation, and Seoul’s redevelopment supply data available as of August 16, 2026.",
    commentary: {
      title: "Housing Supply Should Be a Record of Delivery, Not a Competition of Big Numbers",
      summary: "More important than 230,000 versus 310,000 is how many homes are actually added where people want to live, at what price and when they can move in.",
      paragraphs: ["The central government’s 230,000-plus and Seoul’s 310,000 are not measured in the same way. One is metropolitan net additional supply; the other is gross construction starts for city renewal projects.", "Seoul’s own estimated net addition is about 87,000 because redevelopment and reconstruction replace existing homes. Gross supply and net additions should always be separated.", "The government’s figure also needs a timetable. Although it describes the 230,000-plus as net additions, only about 120,000-plus is expected to begin construction by 2030. Construction is not occupancy.", "Supply expansion is necessary, but credibility comes from execution rather than the size of an announcement.", "Government and local authorities should explain which neighborhoods receive which types and prices of housing and whether transport, schools and jobs are prepared alongside it.", "Urban renewal can increase supply but may temporarily remove housing through demolition and relocation, affecting nearby rents.", "SEED proposes a common public table showing candidate sites, permits, construction starts, completions, occupancy, demolitions, net additions, delays and cancellations.", "Policy is not complete when a number is announced. It becomes an achievement only when homes are delivered and citizens can actually use them."]
    },
